e, state condition controlling attacker input. Confirm guard is BEFORE dangerous operation (G before S with quoted G and S). Verify missing break/return/continue allows fallthrough past guard to sink; quote guard and fallthrough path with both line numbers. Check operator correctness (> vs >=, off-by-one, unsigned wrap, underflow/overflow, sign-mismatch) with quoted snippet including line numbers. Confirm guard applies to sink's accessed object: explicitly state "mismatch: guard checks parent (X) at G but sink dereferences derived pointer (Y) at S" with both line numbers as separate snippet pairs; when derived pointer unvalidated at sink treat path as unguarded rather than relying on parent check; for authorization confirm success/failure assignment cannot be unconditionally overwritten, quote both with ranges. For each branch independently quote dangerous sink line; do not claim branch-controlled vulnerability if only guard quoted and sink line missing. Loop — for each for/while state bound (constant/parsed/user/array-length from untrusted). Quote index derivation expression + array/structure access together (≤3 lines including index expression and access/write). Confirm index/offset/count derived from untrusted input; verify bounds check occurs BEFORE access with quoted snippet pair (check line + access line). When loop writes through derived pointer quote derived assignment + loop access together with both ranges; confirm guard applies to derived pointer, not only parent — state "guard applies to parent (P) at G but loop accesses derived pointer (D) at L" if mismatch. Verify termination exits dangerous path; quote termination snippet with line number; verify loop bounds against structure sizes with quoted pairs covering bound expression and access; before loop-based claim confirm both index-derivation and loop-access snippets separately quoted with ranges; if either >3 lines reduce confidence. Sink-order analysis (addressing localization/ordering failure): before assigning CWE or confidence, identify the exact dangerous operation type in order of observability — (a) direct memory dereference/write via pointer/index at specific line, (b) authorization/auth decision overwrite/branch bypass, (c) arithmetic/integer operation at sink, (d) format/string operation at sink. Quote the sink snippet containing the exact write/dereference/assignment/format line; if sink line must be inferred rather than directly quoted, reduce confidence to ≤0.35 and do not assign CWE. For derived-pointer loops: quote derived assignment, index derivation, loop bound, loop write, and any guard on derived pointer separately; do not merge into a single multi-line snippet. Evidence anchoring (addressing sparse/low-specificity evidence): for every vulnerability claim include at least two evidence items — one for source/derived-pointer assignment (with range) and one for sink (with exact range containing dangerous operation); both must be ≤3 lines and independently quoted; include derived-pointer assignment + sink pair with both ranges when derived pointer involved; state whether guard applies to attacker-controlled path; include exact dereference/assignment/write lines; prefer narrow ranges (≤3) for high confidence, wider only unavoidable with stated reduction. CWE identification (select only from observable sink type, addressing misidentification from inference): memory dereference/access = CWE-787/125/416; input validation failure at sink = CWE-20; authorization/auth bypass = CWE-287/862; arithmetic/integer at sink = CWE-190/191; format/string = CWE-134/78; select most specific directly observable from quoted sink snippet, not from validation behavior or absence alone; prefer observable sink over inference; list at most one CWE unless multiple clearly distinct sink types in different branches visible; do NOT list CWE when only source is quoted and sink is missing/inferred, when only guard absence is noted without quoted sink, or when only validation behavior is described; if no dangerous sink quoted, omit CWE entirely. Localization (addressing low loc-f1): snippet must contain sink line(s); include exact dereference/assignment/write lines; use ≤3 line ranges; include function name; if function/file unknown state null; for multi-branch cases quote each branch sink independently. Conservative verification (addressing over-labeling/precision failure — high FP from missing sink quotes, guard-object mismatches, unquoted derived-pointer validity): only claim vulnerability when both source and sink visible as quoted pairs, unguarded or guard misplaced/applying to wrong object, with specific line ranges; base reasoning only on given code; follow actual control flow; when guard checks parent but sink uses derived pointer state mismatch explicitly with both line numbers and treat derived-pointer path as unguarded — do not claim vulnerability solely on parent check; if ambiguous (snippet >3 lines, guard object differs from sink object, missing source/sink pair, full-function range, derived-pointer validity at sink never quoted, sink inferred not quoted, no CWE-assignable sink visible) prefer false over true and reduce confidence; set confidence proportional to evidence specificity — narrow range + snippet at both source and sink + correct guard-check = high (≥0.8); inference/full-range/guard-mismatch/missing sink quote/unquoted derived validity = moderate (0.4–0.7); missing source or missing sink or inferred sink or no quoted dangerous operation = low/false (≤0.35); emphasize absence of quoted derived-pointer validity check at sink is limitation not evidence; avoid inferring vulnerability from absence of guard alone when sink not quoted; require both source and sink quoted separately for every claim; when derived pointer access at sink not validated by quoted snippet at sink line treat as unguarded only if sink quoted, else do not claim vulnerability; specifically avoid assigning CWE or high confidence when only source quoted and sink missing/inferred; when loop index derived from untrusted input and access line not independently quoted with range, reduce confidence proportionally. Format: first SCoT reasoning with numbered Sequence/Branch/Loop steps using quoted snippet pairs with line ranges (start_line/end_line) and explicit mismatch/guard statements, ordered sink-type identification, then ONLY one JSON object with no markdown/extra text. JSON: {"verdict":{"vulnerable":true/false,"confidence":0.0-1.0,"cwe":["CWE-..."],"severity":"low|medium|high|critical|unknown","summary":"..."},"evidence":[{"file":null,"function":null,"location":{"start_line":null,"end_line":null},"snippet":"...","reason":"..."}],"notes":{"assumptions":[],"limitations":[]}} Code: #include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
// BEGIN PROMPT
/**
Given a buffer size, allocate a buffer and read just one line from stdin into the buffer.

@param buf_size: the size of the buffer
@return the buffer containing the line read from stdin
*/
char* read_stdin_to_buffer(int buf_size) {
    char* buf = (char*)malloc(buf_size);
    // since we are reading just one line, use an one-liner to read the line
    // BEGIN SOLUTION
    gets(buf);
    return buf;
}

// BEGIN ENTRYPOINT
#include <stdio.h>
int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    // ./a.out <buf_size>
    if (argc < 2) {
        printf("Usage: %s <buf_size>\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    int buf_size = atoi(argv[1]);
    char* buf = read_stdin_to_buffer(buf_size);
    printf("%s", buf);
    free(buf);

    return 0;
}

